{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527290122,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83ee1ffeYEdnoBAHqwXnbt","part":{"partID":"prt_1d229625d002yfGhB0WkRrNGrx","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83ee1ffeYEdnoBAHqwXnbt","messageID":"msg_1d2295375001TZtXefwo2y0mrT","type":"tool","id":"call_5991cb94e01844d6b9adc269","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls .skilled/skills/sk-code/ 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,60p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md 2>/dev/null"},"output":"benchmark\nchangelog\ndescription.json\nfeature-catalog\ngraph-metadata.json\nhub-router.json\nleaf-manifest.json\nmanual-testing-playbook\nmode-registry.json\nREADME.md\nROUTER.md\nshared\nsk-code-ci\nsk-code-frontend\nsk-code-quality\nsk-code-review\nsk-code-skilled\nSKILL.md\n---\ntitle: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\ndescription: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n  - \"surface resource maps\"\n  - \"intent classification routing\"\n  - \"load tier resource loading\"\n  - \"unknown surface fallback\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.3.19.0\nrouter_state: active\nskill_pointer: SKILL.md\n---\n# sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n\nLeaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nThe smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n- When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n- When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n- When picking verification commands for the surface\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRouting is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n- `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n\n---\n\n## 2. INTENT MODEL\n\nIntent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n\n### Scoring algorithm\n\n1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n3. Select the top intent.\n4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n\n### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"benchmark\nchangelog\ndescription.json\nfeature-catalog\ngraph-metadata.json\nhub-router.json\nleaf-manifest.json\nmanual-testing-playbook\nmode-registry.json\nREADME.md\nROUTER.md\nshared\nsk-code-ci\nsk-code-frontend\nsk-code-quality\nsk-code-review\nsk-code-skilled\nSKILL.md\n---\ntitle: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\ndescription: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n  - \"surface resource maps\"\n  - \"intent classification routing\"\n  - \"load tier resource loading\"\n  - \"unknown surface fallback\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.3.19.0\nrouter_state: active\nskill_pointer: SKILL.md\n---\n# sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n\nLeaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nThe smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n- When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n- When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n- When picking verification commands for the surface\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRouting is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n\n### Key Sources\n\n- [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n- [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n- `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n\n---\n\n## 2. INTENT MODEL\n\nIntent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n\n### Scoring algorithm\n\n1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n3. Select the top intent.\n4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n\n### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"ls .skilled/skills/sk-code/ 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,60p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md 2>/dev/null\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527289949,"end":1794527290122}}}}
